#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364803 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd13fafc-d765-5e44-aa5a-79c2fff71212" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution With Inverse Hyperbolic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03ce8b69-a178-5bce-9f24-76b01c0c8615" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\ln(2+\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03ce8b69-a178-5bce-9f24-76b01c0c8615" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\ln(2+\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364805} :answer-field/id #uuid "981baf1d-c3e3-5884-8c17-781894d4c162" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "163f3213-d2ff-59ff-ac09-5c275911ad5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-346522" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}}\\,\\text{d}x=4{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse hyperbolic sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=3\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\cdot (3\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =12{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}} \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡u{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡2-12\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡0 \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡2 \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\sqrt{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364806 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "25d92a33-95e8-5860-b461-8291dac502d6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution With Inverse Hyperbolic Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22639c2a-99d4-56c6-8f7b-345921c76974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡{e}^{2x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364808} {:answer/id #uuid "f08d239f-7515-50fd-a4ca-e3b033353984" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡{e}^{2x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364809} {:answer/id #uuid "805a929d-1376-529b-a9cf-a8bfb8187ab4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡{e}^{2x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364810} {:answer/id #uuid "78892773-f66a-5642-8f4e-7193c730fa52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364811} {:answer/id #uuid "79b5d26e-d826-5762-befb-9c6cac781a3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364812}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78892773-f66a-5642-8f4e-7193c730fa52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364811} :answer-field/id #uuid "d00a9ef1-3fed-5b2b-a870-3f1e2d688206" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1cc17f63-e50c-556b-8900-7b2cf2330aa9" :question/math-academy-id "q-95689" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{{e}^{x}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2x}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic cosine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${e}^{2x}={({e}^{x})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u={e}^{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{e}^{x}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2x}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{{e}^{x}}{\\sqrt{{({e}^{x})}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{u}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡{e}^{x}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364813 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "25d92a33-95e8-5860-b461-8291dac502d6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution With Inverse Hyperbolic Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b6b538c-5cd6-5014-bf3d-9088dd407610" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364815} {:answer/id #uuid "13a714cb-376f-51e3-bcd5-7006f4f8b2c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364816} {:answer/id #uuid "70ec86c4-2d0e-5111-bd64-944071d3aea2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364817} {:answer/id #uuid "c18c5c11-1fb8-5a40-97aa-db605cff8b5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364818} {:answer/id #uuid "5e5a756d-8edd-5bed-b027-235403d859a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70ec86c4-2d0e-5111-bd64-944071d3aea2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364817} :answer-field/id #uuid "50d08cb4-7667-5c0a-91c0-046da47555d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bf611d2f-e981-5172-9028-3aadb77e03fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-95852" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-16}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-16}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(\\frac{x}{4})}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic cosine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{4})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{4}.$ Differentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=4\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-16}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(\\frac{x}{4})}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364820 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5aabe1c9-7a37-5b34-893f-9338f5c7c69a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution With Inverse Hyperbolic Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eef343b1-105f-5162-8a40-15409cd4083e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364822} {:answer/id #uuid "bf0bceb5-7701-56a9-8aff-1e53a7c45008" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{artanh}⁡9x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364823} {:answer/id #uuid "25ae9629-bba3-585e-9c97-44a26b563190" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{artanh}⁡9x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364824} {:answer/id #uuid "035f4c43-6a04-5c0c-a52c-855d2dbec9ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{artanh}⁡3x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364825} {:answer/id #uuid "e0187173-3def-55d2-944e-faafe3131d2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "035f4c43-6a04-5c0c-a52c-855d2dbec9ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{artanh}⁡3x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364825} :answer-field/id #uuid "029b2f45-bd43-55bf-b8dd-3185e603ad55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a6accc57-b07f-5510-94f9-9ac61facd7d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-95861" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{3}{1-9{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $9{x}^{2}=(3x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=3x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{1-9{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{1-(3x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{artanh}⁡3x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition $|3x|<1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364827 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd13fafc-d765-5e44-aa5a-79c2fff71212" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution With Inverse Hyperbolic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f33b98f8-704d-550d-94e6-3ae2732ceb0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364829} {:answer/id #uuid "effcfe11-e46c-5761-979b-61c1beacb738" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364830} {:answer/id #uuid "b687ecca-57d2-50b6-ba5c-80b00c28c5ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364831} {:answer/id #uuid "1edc33de-5d8c-5e45-b509-c8ae038c4f68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364832} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a33444-8144-5776-b2bb-0cb602dd17f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2a33444-8144-5776-b2bb-0cb602dd17f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364833} :answer-field/id #uuid "20af7538-411f-5514-ad23-24a32a255af7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "280f0812-dc1a-57cc-8ada-d4af8d29cbec" :question/math-academy-id "q-95883" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1/12}\\frac{2}{1-9{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1/12}\\frac{2}{1-9{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{1/12}\\frac{1}{1-(3x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $(3x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=3x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{12}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/12}\\frac{1}{1-9{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1/12}\\frac{1}{1-(3x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1/4}\\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1/4}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1/4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{4})-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
